      "notes": "Verdict is coherent and well-reasoned. The 'worsens/minor/low' verdict on O10 is defensible: political donations as political expression is an established liberty argument, the restriction plainly removes discretion above the cap, and the magnitude is appropriately kept minor given it binds only on very large donors. All claims are within their stated tiers \u2014 E4 and E5 are measurable baselines, E40 and E37 are correctly projected. The quote from E37 ('pretty generous') is narrow and the claim around it accurately characterises it as illustrative of the cap-level spectrum rather than overstating it. Advocacy sources (ERS, Fair Vote UK, TI UK) are used only for contextual baseline data and cap-level debate, not to drive the liberty verdict itself, which rests on a structural argument. No party identity or motive attribution appears. The 'too-uncertain' rule is not triggered (direction is 'worsens'). No false balance issue: the verdict doesn't manufacture a counter-side, it simply argues a real but narrow worsening. One minor observation: E5 cites Transparency International UK's '3,000 years' comparison as measurable, but it is an advocacy framing; the underlying donation figure (\u00a39m) is measurable while the framing is advocacy colour \u2014 this is low-risk given it is not load-bearing for the verdict direction or magnitude."
    },
